---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import shedPhoto from '../assets/photos/shed-solar-array.jpg';
import soilTrench from '../assets/photos/soil-trench-bedrock.jpg';
import soilMeter from '../assets/photos/soil-ph-meter.jpg';
import soilSurface from '../assets/photos/soil-surface.jpg';
import trenchRun from '../assets/photos/trench-run.jpg';
import tractorTrenching from '../assets/photos/tractor-trenching.jpg';
import logsStaged from '../assets/photos/logs-staged.jpg';
import waterTanks from '../assets/photos/water-tanks-solar.jpg';
import barnTractor from '../assets/photos/barn-tractor.jpg';

const rowSizes = '(max-width: 44rem) 100vw, 24rem';
---
<Base
  title="The Land: soil, water and the plan | Windrose Farm"
  description="37 acres of Arizona high desert at 5,700 feet: bedrock at 8 to 20 inches, alkaline soil, about 12 inches of rain, and the five-phase plan for growing food on it."
  image={soilTrench}
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>The land</h1>
    <p>
      What we're working with, what has been done to it so far, and what is
      planned. Last updated November 2026.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Where</dt><dd>Between Heber and Holbrook, in Navajo County, Arizona</dd>
        <dt>Size</dt><dd>37 acres</dd>
        <dt>Elevation</dt><dd>About 5,700 ft, falling roughly 40 ft from east to west across the developed area</dd>
        <dt>Rain</dt><dd>Roughly 12 inches a year, most of it in the summer monsoon</dd>
        <dt>Season</dt><dd>Roughly 120 to 140 frost-free days</dd>
        <dt>Winters</dt><dd>Cold</dd>
        <dt>Soil</dt><dd>Shallow and alkaline, over rock</dd>
      </dl>
      <p class="note">
        Soil figures on this page come from the NRCS Web Soil Survey. Rain
        and season length are estimates until we have our own records.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Soil</h2>
      <p>
        The NRCS Web Soil Survey maps most of the property as two soil
        complexes, Leanto-Bisoodi and Mellenthin-Rock outcrop. In practice
        that means bedrock 8 to 20 inches down, very slow infiltration
        (hydrologic group D), a lot of calcium carbonate, and roughly 1.2 to
        1.7 inches of available water. NRCS quotes that last figure for a
        standard 60-inch profile, and here most of that depth is rock.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the hand we were dealt, so we plan around it. The idea is to
        build soil on top of the ground with hügelkultur mounds, swales,
        mulch and compost, instead of assuming there's soil ready to plant
        into.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we've found on the ground is a little better than the map, at
        least near the buildings. The water-line trench is about two feet
        deep and never hit rock. The rock outcrop starts about 100 yards east
        of the machine shed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our own testing so far amounts to that trench and a handheld pH
        meter. A lab soil test is still to come.
      </p>
      <div class="photo-row cols-3">
        <Photo src={soilTrench} sizes={rowSizes} alt="A trench wall in red soil, with a harder, rockier layer beneath a few inches of loose soil">
          The soil profile in a trench wall: loose red soil on top, a harder
          and rockier layer below it.
        </Photo>
        <Photo src={soilSurface} sizes={rowSizes} alt="A dry shrub growing in bare red soil covered with small gravel">
          The surface. Red, dry and gravelly.
        </Photo>
        <Photo src={soilMeter} sizes={rowSizes} alt="A handheld soil meter pushed into dry red soil, its display reading 8.2 pH">
          A handheld meter reading pH 8.2, May 2026.
        </Photo>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Water</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Well and storage.</strong> One well, 775 feet deep, giving
          about 6 gallons a minute of alkaline water. A solar-powered
          Grundfos pump lifts it into two 2,500-gallon tanks. The plumbing is
          being rebuilt, and
          <a href="/journal/rebuilding-the-well-plumbing/">the journal has that story</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Irrigation</strong> is designed to run from the tanks on
          timers and not directly off the well. For now the farm is tended
          on weekends, so everything has to survive two weeks without a
          visit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Swales</strong> are planned on contour, across the
          east-to-west slope, to catch runoff and give it time to soak in
          around the planting areas. None are cut yet. Where they go comes
          from <a href="/journal/reading-the-water/">the water map</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hügelkultur mounds</strong> are planned as raised beds over
          the shallow soil: logs soaked, then buried under native soil, to
          hold moisture. The logs are ponderosa pine and juniper that the
          previous owners cut in a nearby forest and left here. The first
          mound will be a prototype between the barn and the machine shed,
          close to the logs and to water. What we learn from it will shape
          the larger system along the orchard edge.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rainwater catchment.</strong> Eventually every roof feeds
          a tank.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="photo-row cols-3">
        <Photo src={trenchRun} sizes={rowSizes} alt="A narrow trench cut in a straight line through red soil and dry grass">
          The water-line trench, March 2026. The previous owners started
          it and we're finishing it.
        </Photo>
        <Photo src={tractorTrenching} sizes={rowSizes} alt="A small orange tractor with a backhoe parked beside a trench in red soil">
          The Kubota finishing the trench, May 2026.
        </Photo>
        <Photo src={logsStaged} sizes={rowSizes} alt="Ponderosa and juniper logs lying on open ground">
          The previous owners' logs, set aside for the hügelkultur mounds.
        </Photo>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Orchard site and planting list</h2>
      <p>
        The orchard will go in a former horse exercise area. It is flat,
        upslope of the barn and shed, and next to the water system, with a
        swale planned along its uphill edge. The ground is probably compacted
        from years of horses, so it will need subsoiling before planting. It
        may also be more fertile than the rest, thanks to years of manure.
        Planting is planned for spring 2027, with the trees on or beside the
        hügelkultur berms.
      </p>
      <p>The planting list so far, chosen for alkaline soil, dry air and cold winters:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Asparagus.</strong> The most alkaline-tolerant crop on the list, productive for 15 to 20 years, and the earliest harvest of the year.</li>
        <li><strong>Jujube.</strong> Tolerates alkaline soil, drought, cold winters and hot summers, and can live more than 50 years.</li>
        <li><strong>Apples.</strong> Cold-hardy varieties on alkaline-tolerant rootstock.</li>
        <li><strong>Herbs and alliums.</strong> An understory beneath the trees.</li>
        <li><strong>Lavender.</strong> Likely. It likes alkaline, well-drained soil, and feeds bees.</li>
        <li><strong>Berries.</strong> Elderberry, haskap and currants, planned for 2028.</li>
        <li><strong>Windbreak and woodlot.</strong> Arizona cypress, northern catalpa and thornless honey locust, planned for 2028.</li>
        <li><strong>Wild harvest.</strong> Prickly pear and pinyon pine already grow here without any help.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Buildings, power and equipment</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Machine shed.</strong> Standing, and the working center of the property.</li>
        <li><strong>Barn.</strong> Standing, with the slab and interior still in progress.</li>
        <li><strong>Solar.</strong> The well pump is solar-powered. Off-grid power for the buildings starts at the machine shed: an EG4 18kPV inverter and a 48V all-weather battery bank, with a buried feeder planned out to the barn.</li>
        <li><strong>Septic.</strong> A permitted septic system is already in.</li>
        <li><strong>Tractor.</strong> A Kubota BX23S for trenching, subsoiling, grading and building mounds.</li>
        <li><strong>Internet.</strong> Starlink.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="photo-row cols-3">
        <Photo src={shedPhoto} sizes={rowSizes} alt="The red machine shed with solar panels along its green metal roof">
          The machine shed, with solar panels on the roof.
        </Photo>
        <Photo src={waterTanks} sizes={rowSizes} alt="Two black water storage tanks beside a timber-framed solar array in open grassland">
          The storage tanks, beside a timber-framed solar array.
        </Photo>
        <Photo src={barnTractor} sizes={rowSizes} alt="An orange compact tractor parked inside the open barn">
          The Kubota BX23S in the barn.
        </Photo>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The plan</h2>
      <p>The rules we're working by:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Water before biology.</strong> No planting until the water is boringly dependable.</li>
        <li><strong>Mounds are mandatory.</strong> They build the root zone the soil doesn't have.</li>
        <li><strong>Fence the growing area first,</strong> and the perimeter later, in phases.</li>
        <li><strong>Set-and-forget over high-touch.</strong> Crops and systems have to get by on weekend visits.</li>
        <li><strong>Rodent-proof everything we build.</strong></li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The order follows the dependencies: mounds, a swale and fencing
        first, then the orchard and lavender, then bees. These are plans,
        and the dates will move if the water isn't ready.
      </p>
      <dl class="status">
        <dt>2026</dt>
        <dd>General cleanup. Rebuilding the well plumbing and tanks. <span class="tag">In progress</span></dd>
        <dt>Winter 2027</dt>
        <dd>
          Start baseline monitoring: a rain gauge, soil-moisture sensors in a
          mound and in a control bed, photo points, and a log of how much
          water the well delivers. Join the University of Arizona Cooperative
          Extension Master Gardener program.
        </dd>
        <dt>Spring 2027</dt>
        <dd>
          Rip the orchard rows. Plant asparagus and the orchard core of
          jujube and apples, with an herb and allium understory, on drip
          irrigation from the tanks. Cut the first swale on the orchard's uphill edge.
        </dd>
        <dt>Monsoon 2027</dt>
        <dd>Watch and record how water moves, and adjust the earthworks.</dd>
        <dt>Also 2027</dt>
        <dd>Start building a small off-grid cabin, about 12 by 24 feet.</dd>
        <dt>2028</dt>
        <dd>
          The apiary, inside a bear-proof electric fence. The windbreak and
          woodlot. The berry layer. Perimeter fencing, in phases and
          wildlife-friendly.
        </dd>
        <dt>2029 to 2036</dt>
        <dd>
          Every roof feeds a tank. Hard cider and fruit wine. A greenhouse,
          kitchen garden and aquaponics once someone is living on site. Full
          off-grid energy. Field days and demonstration projects.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What has gone wrong so far</h2>
      <p>
        Time. We don't have enough of it. Nobody lives here yet, and so far
        the work has been cleanup and the well plumbing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The plumbing we inherited. The joints had failed and rodents had
        gotten into the tanks, so the first big job was one we hadn't
        planned on.
      </p>
      <p>
        The neighboring ranch's cattle also get in, and have chewed through
        at least one camera cord. Fencing is on the list.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="next-step">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="btn" href="/farm/">Plans for the farm →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
